        </div><div class="article-template__content page-width page-width--narrow rte scroll-trigger animate--slide-in">
          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        A tan fades as your skin renews itself, and what helps is daily sunscreen and gentle pigment-fighting ingredients, while popular home remedies like lemon juice can make things worse.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            A tan cannot be scrubbed or bleached off overnight, because the pigment sits inside your skin cells and fades as skin renews. What helps is daily broad-spectrum sunscreen, to stop new pigment forming, along with a gentle ingredient such as niacinamide used for several weeks. Skip lemon juice and harsh scrubs, since citrus on sun-exposed skin has caused burn-like blistering and dark marks, and irritation can leave marks of its own.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Few skin concerns attract as many home remedies as a tan. Lemon, baking soda, gram flour and potato all promise to lift it, and plenty of people try them after a holiday. Once you know what a tan actually is, it becomes clear why most of them fall short.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What a tan actually is
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A tan is your skin's defence against sun exposure. It makes extra pigment, called melanin, and that pigment sits inside your skin cells. You cannot wash or scrub it off, because it is part of living skin. It fades only as the skin renews and the tanned cells are replaced.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Ultraviolet rays are not the only trigger. Visible light can also add pigment, particularly in darker skin tones, and an ordinary broad-spectrum sunscreen does not cover it well.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a> That is one reason dark marks can hang around even when you are careful about UV.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Home remedies to skip
    </h2>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Lemon juice and other citrus.</strong> Citrus fruit contains compounds that react with UV-A light. Doctors have reported a child who squeezed lemon juice onto her skin in strong sunshine and was admitted with burn-like blistering, a reaction that can leave dark marks behind.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29324427/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a> It is one reported case, but it shows how risky the combination is.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Hard scrubbing.</strong> A tan is inside the cells, so scrubbing cannot lift it. It irritates the skin instead, and irritation can leave dark marks of its own, known as post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34525885/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Baking soda, toothpaste and other DIY pastes.</strong> There is no research behind them as tan treatments, and they can irritate skin.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A good rule is that if a remedy stings or burns, it is not working in your favour.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">
