    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>

    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How long does a tan take to fade?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">It fades gradually as your skin renews itself, and how long it takes depends on how deep the tan is and on your skin tone. Sunscreen stops it from deepening in the meantime. Marks that are a few shades darker than your natural colour usually fade within 6 to 12 months once the cause is stopped.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Does lemon juice remove a tan?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No, and it carries a real risk. Citrus on sun-exposed skin can trigger a phototoxic reaction that causes blistering followed by dark marks.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29324427/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I scrub a tan off?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No. The pigment is inside the skin cells, so scrubbing cannot lift it, and the irritation it causes can leave dark marks of its own.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34525885/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is tinted sunscreen worth it?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">If you have dark marks or melasma, yes. Iron oxide protects against visible light, which is a cause of dark spots, and dermatologists recommend tinted sunscreen with iron oxide for caring for them.<a href="https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/routine/fade-dark-spots" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>4</sup></a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32335182/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>5</sup></a></p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I use a brightening serum on sunburnt skin?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Wait until the burn has settled. Skin that is red, hot or peeling is already irritated, and adding an active can make that worse.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">When should I see a dermatologist?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">See one if marks keep getting darker, spread, change shape or do not improve after several months of sunscreen and gentle care. Dermatologists can also advise on stronger treatments such as retinoids and hydroxy acids.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34525885/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        References
    </h2>
